sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch06-07> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Natural selection could have converted a simple optic nerve coated with pigment and covered by transparent membrane into a highly perfect eye through a series of graded steps, each useful to its possessor, as shown by the graduated diversity of eyes in living crustaceans.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Complex eyes can be built by selection through graded useful steps";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter VI, “Difficulties on Theory”; section “Organs of extreme perfection.”. Scope: eyes of Articulata (arthropods and allies), extended to vertebrates"
}
